<description>
Swing an irregular line segment around a nearby obstacle sprite, preserving its pixel count.

Input structure:
- bg, bar color (straight lines perpendicular to the grid edge, one end on the border), segment color (irregular stroke attached to bar's inner endpoint), obstacle color (sprite near the bar terminal).

Rule:
- Each bar is perpendicular to the edge it touches; its inner (terminal) endpoint has an attached irregular segment.
- The segment detaches and re-routes: it travels from the terminal toward the nearest obstacle, hugs the obstacle's boundary, then exits at a 45° diagonal.
- Total painted pixels equal the original segment length.

Key insight: The segment behaves like a rope swung around the obstacle — attach at the nearest point, trace the skin, exit diagonally, conserving length.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="2" source="train" puzzle_id="60d73be6" lines="22-39">
<description>
Complete the sparse colored marker pattern by reflecting it across a full-row/full-column cross.

Input structure
The grid has a dominant background and one non-background cross color that
fills exactly one complete row and one complete column. All other foreground
pixels are sparse markers away from that cross.

Rule
Keep the input unchanged, and for each marker pixel copy its color to the
positions obtained by reflecting it across the cross row, across the cross
column, and across both axes. Reflected positions outside the grid are
clipped.

Key insight
The full cross is not a copied object; it is the pair of mirror axes for
completing the quadrant pattern.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="3" source="train" puzzle_id="83eb0a57" lines="72-86">
<description>
Crop the largest object and overlay each smaller sprite where its shared marker pattern matches.

Input structure
- The background is the most common color, and the foreground has one largest destination object plus smaller source sprites.
- Each source sprite shares exactly one marker color with the destination object.

Rule
- Output the tight bbox crop of the largest object.
- Translate each source sprite so every connected marker component of the shared color exactly matches destination marker components.
- Paste source sprites in decreasing area order, so smaller sprites can sit on top of larger ones when they overlap.

Key insight
- The shared-color marker components are alignment keys; matching whole marker components distinguishes singleton slots from larger same-color marker blocks.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="4" source="train" puzzle_id="9f669b64" lines="26-48">
<description>
Push the central solid rectangle to the grid edge, splitting the perpendicular target rectangle around its path.

Input structure
- The background is the dominant color.
- There are exactly three monochrome foreground objects arranged on one
  horizontal or vertical axis.
- The middle object on that axis is a solid rectangular moving object.
- One end object is a solid rectangle or bar whose long side is perpendicular
  to the movement axis; this is the target to split.

Rule
- Move the middle rectangle along the axis toward the target until it is
  flush with the grid edge in that direction.
- Clear the original middle rectangle and the original target.
- Split the target through the moving rectangle's two-cell-wide path and
  shift the two halves one cell outward perpendicular to the movement.
- Keep the other end object unchanged.

Key insight
- The non-target end object acts as a pusher; the central rectangle travels
  away from it through the perpendicular target, opening a matching gap.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

<reference_puzzle index="5" source="train" puzzle_id="320afe60" lines="41-56">
<description>
Slide each foreground object horizontally to the edge indicated by whether its shape has an open notch.

Input structure:
- Yellow is the background and there is one blue foreground color.
- The foreground is split into separated 4-connected objects.
- Each object keeps its row span and shape in the output.

Rule:
- If an object's bbox border contains a background gap flanked by object pixels along that same border, slide it to the right edge and recolor it green.
- Otherwise slide the object to the left edge and recolor it red.
- Start from a blank background canvas; no moved objects overlap.

Key insight:
- The target side is encoded by the object's shape, not by its original position: open notches go right, while closed or solid shapes go left.
</description>
</reference_puzzle>

